<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lamu Old Town</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lamu Old Town is the historic centre of Lamu, on Lamu Island off the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>. Inhabited for more than seven centuries, it is the oldest and best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, with narrow streets, coral-stone houses with carved wooden doors, and mosques. It was a major port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lamu-old-town.jpg" alt="Lamu Old Town" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Almaddy2022 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamu_Old_Town.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lamu is a small town on Lamu Island, part of the Lamu Archipelago of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, reached by crossing a sea channel from Mokowe Jetty, the end of the road that runs 341 kilometres north-east from Mombasa. The original name was Amu, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> rendered Al-Amu; the Portuguese called it Lamon and applied the name to the whole island. The town is believed to have been established in 1370 and is Kenya's oldest continually inhabited town, one of the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> settlements along the East African coast. It was first attested in writing by the Arab traveller Abu-al-Mahasini, who met a judge from Lamu visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1441.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1506 a Portuguese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristao-da-cunha">Tristão da Cunha</a> sent a ship to blockade Lamu, and its king had to agree to pay an annual tribute and 600 meticals at once. Portugal held a monopoly of shipping along the East African coast and taxed exports. In the 1580s, prompted by Turkish raids, Lamu led a rebellion against the Portuguese, and in 1652 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> helped it resist their control.</p><p>From the late seventeenth to the early nineteenth century Lamu was an Omani protectorate, and this is regarded as its golden age. It was governed as a republic by a council of elders called the Yumbe, and became a centre of poetry, politics, arts, crafts and trade, with many buildings in a distinct classical style. The poet Mwana Kupona, author of Advice on the Wifely Duty, had a higher status there than convention elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> allowed.</p><p>In 1812 a coalition Pate-Mazrui army landed at Shela and was violently repulsed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shela">Battle of Shela</a>; Lamu then asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> for a Busaidi garrison at its new fort. Fumo Madi ibn Abi Bakr, sultan of Pate, began the fort and died in 1809, and it was finished by the early 1820s.</p><p>The town came under the political influence of the sultan of Zanzibar in the mid-nineteenth century, and a German post office operated there from 22 November 1888 to 3 March 1891. In 1890 Lamu came under British rule under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a>. Its economy rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> until abolition in 1907, and the Uganda Railroad, begun at Mombasa in 1901, further hampered it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lamu Old Town was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2001 as the oldest and best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Most of its people are Muslim, and it has 23 mosques; the island has no motorised vehicles, and about 3,000 donkeys do the carrying. The Maulidi Festival celebrates the birth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lamu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
